<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="54367ec7">&lt;p&gt;
    The Objectives, Deliverables, Workplan (ODW) technique is a simple, but effective way of managing the alignment of a
    project workplan with the overall objectives of the project. This is done by tracing the relationship of Objectives to
    Deliverables, and to the Workplan tasks that are required to deliver the stated outcomes and outputs of the project so
    that the project plan encompasses the right activities and to task to meet the project objectives. It also contributes
    to more robust project charter development and scope statement definition.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Often, project managers and teams build project plans with too much emphasis on defining &quot;top of mind&quot; activities as if
    they were building a large group &quot;To Do&quot; list. At other times, planners will fall back to a previous existing workplan
    and modify it for the situation at hand. There are several risks in these situations. First, the team may miss a set of
    required activities to meet project objectives. Second, they may add tasks that consume time and energy, but do not
    directly contribute to the progress of the project. The ODW (Objectives-Deliverables-Workplan) technique mitigates
    these risks by tracing all workplan tasks back to the deliverables that need to be created, and how these deliverables
    will satisfy the project objectives. Once the initial ODW check is done during project inititiation, it should be
    repeated if major project plan revisions occur in order to maintain the alignment of objectives, deliverables and the
    workplan.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The key questions to be asked are summarized below:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;./../../../core.mgmt.common.base/guidances/concepts/./resources/PM-600-0012.png&quot; width=&quot;600&quot; height=&quot;292&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In the first step, business objectives need to be translated into specific project objectives. They are not the same in
    all cases. The project objectives should define what the project alone will actually achieve and be evaluated against.
    In the second step, project deliverables may be tangible products and services or outcomes. In the final step, the a
    single, deliverable-oriented Work Breakdown Structure (WBS) should be defined that includes all the deliverables of the
    project, This hierachical decomposition of deliverables is then transformed into a work plan. From a scope standpoint,
    only those items within the WBS are considered in-scope for the project.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Use of the ODW technique will maintain alignment and integrity of the workplan against the client’s needs to avoid the
    risk of producing a meaningless plan of unfocused activities. This information is also a very clear way to communicate
    project scope and how the desired outcomes and deliverables with be achieved.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
